      "source": [
        "# Week 8 Demo  \n",
        "## Predicting Energy Demand Using the UCI Appliance Energy Dataset\n",
        "\n",
        "In this demo, we use the **UCI Appliance Energy Prediction dataset** to predict appliance energy use in a low-energy house.\n",
        "\n",
        "The target variable is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Appliances\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a **supervised learning regression** problem because we use input features to predict a numerical energy-use value."
      ],
      "id": "Cu_QlZTLuDZ0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "H0fYDq7muDZ1"
      },
      "source": [
        "## What this notebook does\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Downloads the dataset from UCI and fixit. Or the fixed data from course site\n",
        "2. Selects a set of features.\n",
        "3. Trains two models:\n",
        "   - Linear Regression\n",
        "   - Random Forest Regression\n",
        "   - ??\n",
        "5. Compares the models using:\n",
        "   - MAE\n",
        "   - RMSE\n",
        "   - R²\n",
        "6. Plots observed vs predicted appliance energy use.\n",
        "7. Shows feature importance for the Random Forest model."
      ],
      "id": "H0fYDq7muDZ1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cwMElCc-uDZ2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 1: Install and import packages"
      ],
      "id": "cwMElCc-uDZ2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5wT9kj7duDZ2"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Run this cell first to get the libraries that we plan to use.\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
        "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\n",
        "from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, r2_score"
      ],
      "id": "5wT9kj7duDZ2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "wTiZQT7_uDZ3"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 2: Get the UCI Appliance Energy dataset\n",
        "\n",
        "We have two options\n",
        "\n",
        "1.Get it directly from the UCI website and fix it forour use\n",
        "2.Download already fixed dataset from the data link url for this assignment\n",
        "\n",
        "### For option 1\n",
        "The UCI dataset ID for **Appliances Energy Prediction** is `374`.\n",
        "\n",
        "This cell downloads the dataset and combines the input features and target into one table. Note that we had to install another library and call it to get the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "The original dataset has a date column. Machine learning models cannot directly use a date/time string, so we create simple time features:\n",
        "\n",
        "hour of day\n",
        "day of week\n",
        "These features help represent daily and weekly patterns in appliance energy use.\n",
        "\n",
        "Before creating the new features we have to format the \"date\" as it is not in a format that can be easly broken into the parts we want. Dont worry about the code that we are using to change the format. What you need to understand is that code is changing the each data item to a format that we can use.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### For option 2\n",
        "You only need to give the corret url for the csv that has everything fixed\n",
        "\n",
        "for both options make sure you get the data!"
      ],
      "id": "wTiZQT7_uDZ3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "rd0CsAZluDZ3"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# The ucimlrepo package lets us download datasets directly from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.\n",
        "\n",
        "!pip -q install ucimlrepo\n",
        "from ucimlrepo import fetch_ucirepo\n",
        "\n",
        "appliance_data = fetch_ucirepo(id=374)\n",
        "\n",
        "X = appliance_data.data.features\n",
        "y = appliance_data.data.targets\n",
        "\n",
        "df = pd.concat([X, y], axis=1)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Rows and columns:\", df.shape)\n",
        "df.head()\n",
        "\n",
        "df[\"date\"] = df[\"date\"].astype(str)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Insert a space between the date part and time part\n",
        "df[\"date_fixed\"] = df[\"date\"].str.replace(\n",
        "    r\"(\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2})(\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2})\",\n",
        "    r\"\\1 \\2\",\n",
        "    regex=True\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Formating the date filed\n",
        "df[\"date_fixed\"] = pd.to_datetime(\n",
        "    df[\"date_fixed\"],\n",
        "    format=\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "# now we can break it into parts\n",
        "df[\"hour\"] = df[\"date_fixed\"].dt.hour\n",
        "df[\"day_of_week\"] = df[\"date_fixed\"].dt.dayofweek\n",
        "\n",
        "# Inspecting the data\n",
        "df[[\"date\", \"date_fixed\", \"hour\", \"day_of_week\", \"Appliances\"]].head()\n"
      ],
      "id": "rd0CsAZluDZ3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "k4eY5aosuDZ4"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: Option 2: Get the data from the Date Time fixed CSV\n",
        "\n",
        "Only 2 lines compared to option 1!\n",
        "But when you work with data you may have to fix the data before using it on ML models."
      ],
      "id": "k4eY5aosuDZ4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "url = \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UConnARIAL/NRE5615_EnviroAI_Code_and_Data/main/data/Wk8_UCI_Appliance_Energy_DateTimeFixed.csv\"\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(url)\n",
        "df.head()"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KuEpmiOaoBF0"
      },
      "id": "KuEpmiOaoBF0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0cqZ62SLuDZ5"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 3: Inspect the dataset\n",
        "\n",
        "The target variable is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Appliances\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This represents appliance energy use in Wh.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dataset includes indoor temperature, indoor humidity, weather, lighting, and time-related information."
      ],
      "id": "0cqZ62SLuDZ5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Fm3-vK_NuDZ5"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "print(\"Column names:\")\n",
        "print(df.columns.tolist())\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nMissing values:\")\n",
        "print(df.isna().sum().sort_values(ascending=False).head())\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nTarget summary:\")\n",
        "print(df[\"Appliances\"].describe())"
      ],
      "id": "Fm3-vK_NuDZ5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Y0Wzh4qkuDZ5"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 4: Choose features and target\n",
        "\n",
        "For a beginner-friendly model, we use a smaller set of meaningful features.\n",
        "\n",
        "You can later experiment by adding or removing features."
      ],
      "id": "Y0Wzh4qkuDZ5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "j1pDdBFauDZ6"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "target = \"Appliances\"\n",
        "\n",
        "features = [\n",
        "    \"lights\",\n",
        "    \"T1\", \"RH_1\",\n",
        "    \"T2\", \"RH_2\",\n",
        "    \"T3\", \"RH_3\",\n",
        "    \"T_out\",\n",
        "    \"Press_mm_hg\",\n",
        "    \"RH_out\",\n",
        "    \"Windspeed\",\n",
        "    \"hour\",\n",
        "    \"day_of_week\"\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "X = df[features]\n",
        "y = df[target]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Features used:\")\n",
        "print(features)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nTarget:\")\n",
        "print(target)"
      ],
      "id": "j1pDdBFauDZ6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "-YqlLbQiuDZ6"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 5: Split into training and testing data\n",
        "\n",
        "We use a 70/30 split.\n",
        "\n",
        "The model learns from the training data and is evaluated on the testing data.\n",
        "\n",
        "Since we do not make any major model design decisions based on the test set we can do this. But in a case where we decide on the model based on the test set we may want to keep another independent set for final testing. In that case we would split this to 60/20/20. Where we call them train/validation/test."
      ],
      "id": "-YqlLbQiuDZ6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "dIxU9hkOuDZ6"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X, y,\n",
        "    test_size=0.30,\n",
        "    random_state=42\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Training rows:\", X_train.shape[0])\n",
        "print(\"Testing rows:\", X_test.shape[0])"
      ],
      "id": "dIxU9hkOuDZ6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5cmMh-yIuDZ6"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 6: Train two regression models\n",
        "\n",
        "We compare:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Linear Regression** — a simple baseline model.\n",
        "2. **Random Forest Regression** — a more flexible machine learning model.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal is not to build a perfect energy management system. The goal is to compare how different models learn patterns from the same data."
      ],
      "id": "5cmMh-yIuDZ6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "9MT_FhCluDZ6"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "linear_model = LinearRegression()\n",
        "\n",
        "rf_model = RandomForestRegressor(\n",
        "    n_estimators=100,\n",
        "    max_depth=10,\n",
        "    random_state=42,\n",
        "    n_jobs=-1\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "linear_model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "rf_model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Models trained.\")"
      ],
      "id": "9MT_FhCluDZ6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "t2uHOaxCuDZ6"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 7: Evaluate the models\n",
        "\n",
        "We use three common regression metrics:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **MAE**: average absolute error\n",
        "- **RMSE**: larger errors are penalized more strongly\n",
        "- **R²**: how much variation the model explains"
      ],
      "id": "t2uHOaxCuDZ6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qV5E78x1uDZ7"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def evaluate_model(model, X_test, y_test, model_name):\n",
        "    predictions = model.predict(X_test)\n",
        "\n",
        "    mae = mean_absolute_error(y_test, predictions)\n",
        "    rmse = mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions) ** 0.5\n",
        "    r2 = r2_score(y_test, predictions)\n",
        "\n",
        "    return {\n",
        "        \"Model\": model_name,\n",
        "        \"MAE\": mae,\n",
        "        \"RMSE\": rmse,\n",
        "        \"R²\": r2\n",
        "    }, predictions\n",
        "\n",
        "linear_results, linear_predictions = evaluate_model(\n",
        "    linear_model, X_test, y_test, \"Linear Regression\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "rf_results, rf_predictions = evaluate_model(\n",
        "    rf_model, X_test, y_test, \"Random Forest Regression\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "results_df = pd.DataFrame([linear_results, rf_results])\n",
        "results_df"
      ],
      "id": "qV5E78x1uDZ7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "BZB-Ws-zuDZ7"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 8: Plot observed vs predicted values\n",
        "\n",
        "A good model would place points close to the diagonal line.\n",
        "\n",
        "In real environmental and energy data, predictions may not be accurate consideirng the simple models we are using."
      ],
      "id": "BZB-Ws-zuDZ7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "FYKMPvJ9uDZ7"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 6))\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.scatter(y_test, linear_predictions, alpha=0.3, label=\"Linear Regression\")\n",
        "plt.scatter(y_test, rf_predictions, alpha=0.3, label=\"Random Forest Regression\")\n",
        "\n",
        "min_val = min(y_test.min(), linear_predictions.min(), rf_predictions.min())\n",
        "max_val = max(y_test.max(), linear_predictions.max(), rf_predictions.max())\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot([min_val, max_val], [min_val, max_val], linestyle=\"--\", linewidth=1)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Observed appliance energy use (Wh)\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Predicted appliance energy use (Wh)\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Observed vs Predicted Appliance Energy Use\")\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "FYKMPvJ9uDZ7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "WxsyZrpduDZ7"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 9: Feature importance from Random Forest\n",
        "\n",
        "Feature importance gives a simple way to see which variables helped the Random Forest model make predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is useful for interpretation, but it should not be treated as a perfect explanation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Explainable AI and Feature Importance\n",
        "\n",
        "The Random Forest model gives feature importance values, which show which input variables were most useful for predicting appliance energy use. This is a simple example of explainable AI because it helps us understand what the model relied on.\n",
        "\n",
        "If time, lighting, indoor temperature, humidity, or weather variables are highly ranked, that suggests these factors helped the model predict energy demand. However, feature importance does not prove cause and effect. It only shows which variables were useful for prediction in this dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "In energy management, this type of explanation is important because AI results should not be treated as a black box. Managers need to understand which data sources influence the model before using predictions to support decisions."
      ],
      "id": "WxsyZrpduDZ7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "FDxNqKmKuDZ7"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "importance_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"feature\": features,\n",
        "    \"importance\": rf_model.feature_importances_\n",
        "}).sort_values(\"importance\", ascending=False)\n",
        "\n",
        "importance_df"
      ],
      "id": "FDxNqKmKuDZ7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "8tWPVYPouDZ7"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.barh(importance_df[\"feature\"], importance_df[\"importance\"])\n",
        "plt.gca().invert_yaxis()\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Importance\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Feature\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Random Forest Feature Importance\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "8tWPVYPouDZ7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "sj3lSMKtuDZ7"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 10A: Optional: Try a small NN model on the data set\n",
        "\n",
        "Use this optional cell to experiment with a small Neural Network and see if we can improve on the best model we have for now. You can change some of the parameters in the model\n",
        "\n",
        "All models are evaluated on the same held-out test set, so their final performance metrics can be compared. The Neural Network uses a small validation portion of the training data to monitor learning and decide when to stop training. This is a common practice for neural networks. The validation set is not the same as the test set; the test set remains separate and is used only for final evaluation.\n"
      ],
      "id": "sj3lSMKtuDZ7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.neural_network import MLPRegressor\n",
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
        "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "# Small Neural Network with validation tracking\n",
        "nn_model = Pipeline([\n",
        "    (\"scaler\", StandardScaler()),\n",
        "    (\"model\", MLPRegressor(\n",
        "        hidden_layer_sizes=(16,8,8), # Structure of the model\n",
        "        activation=\"relu\",             # How to activate each neoron\n",
        "        solver=\"adam\",                 # How to train the model\n",
        "        learning_rate_init=0.001,\n",
        "        max_iter=1000,\n",
        "        random_state=42,\n",
        "        early_stopping=True,        # Model will stop when there is no improvemnt\n",
        "        validation_fraction=0.20,\n",
        "        n_iter_no_change=20\n",
        "    ))\n",
        "])\n",
        "\n",
        "# Train the Neural Network\n",
        "nn_model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Evaluate using the same evaluate_model() function\n",
        "nn_results, nn_predictions = evaluate_model(\n",
        "    nn_model,\n",
        "    X_test,\n",
        "    y_test,\n",
        "    \"Small Neural Network\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Combine all model results in one comparison table\n",
        "results_df = pd.DataFrame([\n",
        "    linear_results,\n",
        "    rf_results,\n",
        "    nn_results\n",
        "])\n",
        "\n",
        "results_df"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "G0Fhtk7j67l4"
      },
      "id": "G0Fhtk7j67l4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HfREMg5s9Ajd"
      },
      "id": "HfREMg5s9Ajd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ZIdL9l409Lqp"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Step 1OB: Optional: Visualize to see how the model is learning\n",
        "\n",
        "### Interpreting the Neural Network Training Plot\n",
        "\n",
        "This plot shows two views of neural network training.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **training loss** shows how much error the model has while learning from the training data. A lower loss means the model is fitting the training data better.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **validation R² score** shows how well the model performs on a separate validation part of the training data. A higher R² means better prediction performance.\n",
        "\n",
        "Ideally, the training loss should generally decrease, while the validation R² should generally increase or level off. If training loss keeps improving but validation R² gets worse, the model may be overfitting.\n"
      ],
      "id": "ZIdL9l409Lqp"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "mlp = nn_model.named_steps[\"model\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Training loss\n",
        "ax1.plot(mlp.loss_curve_, label=\"Training loss\")\n",
        "ax1.set_xlabel(\"Training iteration\")\n",
        "ax1.set_ylabel(\"Training loss\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Validation R² score\n",
        "ax2 = ax1.twinx()\n",
        "ax2.plot(mlp.validation_scores_, linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Validation R²\")\n",
        "ax2.set_ylabel(\"Validation R² score\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.title(\"Neural Network Training and Validation Curves\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Combine legends from both axes\n",
        "lines_1, labels_1 = ax1.get_legend_handles_labels()\n",
        "lines_2, labels_2 = ax2.get_legend_handles_labels()\n",
        "ax1.legend(lines_1 + lines_2, labels_1 + labels_2, loc=\"best\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "tZ_3CuOK7Fxo"
      },
      "id": "tZ_3CuOK7Fxo",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
